The Security Council,
Recalling its previous resolutions and the statements of its President concerning the Democratic Republic of the Congo, in particular resolutions 1533 (2004), 2666 (2022), 2688 (2023), 2717 (2023), 2738 (2024), 2746 (2024) and 2765 (2024),
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and of all States in the region, and emphasizing the urgent need to respect fully the principles of non-interference, good-neighbourliness and regional cooperation, and reaffirming the sovereignty of the Democratic Republic of the Congo over its natural resources,
Expressing grave concern at the escalation of violence in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo and at the rapid and continuing military offensive by the Mouvement du 23 mars (M23), including the capture of Goma and Bukavu and the advance towards other areas, and condemning in the strongest terms all direct or indirect support to M23, including by Rwandan Defence Forces present on Congolese territory, as documented by the Group of Experts and reported by United Nations officials,
Condemning all armed groups operating in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, including M23, the Allied Democratic Forces, CODECO and the Forces démocratiques de libération du Rwanda (FDLR), and all acts of violence against civilians, including massacres, extrajudicial killings, conflict-related sexual violence, recruitment and use of children, forced displacement, arbitrary detention, and attacks against humanitarian and medical personnel and facilities,
Expressing grave concern at the humanitarian crisis in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo, including mass displacement, refugee flows, loss of life, food insecurity and the obstruction of humanitarian access, and stressing the need for a safe, secure and enabling environment for the delivery of humanitarian assistance,
Condemning in the strongest terms all attacks against United Nations Organization Stabilization Mission in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (MONUSCO) personnel, installations and vehicles, as well as attacks against the Southern African Development Community Mission in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (SAMIDRC) and against humanitarian personnel, and expressing deep concern at the looting of humanitarian facilities, including World Food Programme depots,
Reaffirming the obligations of all parties under international law, including international humanitarian law, international human rights law and international refugee law, as applicable, and emphasizing that those responsible for violations and abuses must be held accountable,
Recognizing the linkage between the illegal exploitation of natural resources, illicit trade in such resources and the proliferation and trafficking of arms as one of the major factors fuelling and exacerbating conflict in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo,
Welcoming the efforts of the African Union, the East African Community, the Southern African Development Community, the International Conference on the Great Lakes Region, Angola as mediator, and the United Nations, including the Special Envoy of the Secretary-General for the Great Lakes Region, and recalling the ceasefire agreement signed by the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Rwanda on 30 July 2024 under the mediation of Angola, as well as the Luanda commitments and the decision to operationalize the FDLR neutralization plan and to elaborate a disengagement plan,
Reaffirming the mandate of MONUSCO, including the protection of civilians, and recalling resolution 2746 (2024), which authorizes MONUSCO to support SAMIDRC through enhanced coordination, information-sharing, technical assistance and the use of MONUSCO’s logistical assets and military capabilities, within the area of MONUSCO’s deployment and consistent with resolution 2717 (2023),
Stressing the importance of coordinated, complementary and effective action by the United Nations, the African Union, the East African Community, the Southern African Development Community and other regional and international partners in support of a lasting political settlement,
Determining that the situation in the Democratic Republic of the Congo continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,
Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,
1. Demands the immediate cessation of all hostilities and the immediate and unconditional withdrawal of M23 from all areas of the Democratic Republic of the Congo that it occupies, including Goma and Bukavu;
2. Demands that the Rwandan Defence Forces immediately cease all support to M23, including the provision of military personnel, weapons, equipment, logistics and operational support, and withdraw from the territory of the Democratic Republic of the Congo without delay;
3. Condemns all direct and indirect support to armed groups operating in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, and demands that all States and actors cease such support, prevent the transfer of arms and related materiel to armed groups, and comply strictly with the sanctions regime established by resolution 1533 (2004) and subsequent resolutions;
4. Demands that all armed groups operating in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, including M23, the Allied Democratic Forces, CODECO and the FDLR, immediately cease all violence, disarm, demobilize and participate in good faith in disarmament, demobilization, reintegration and stabilization processes, and calls for the full implementation of the FDLR neutralization plan;
5. Calls upon all parties to respect international humanitarian law and international human rights law, to protect civilians, including women, children, internally displaced persons and refugees, to prevent and end conflict-related sexual violence and grave violations against children, and to refrain from attacks against civilian objects, including hospitals, schools, water and sanitation facilities, and humanitarian and medical infrastructure;
6. Demands that all parties facilitate safe, timely and unimpeded humanitarian access to all affected populations and ensure the safety and security of humanitarian personnel, medical personnel, humanitarian convoys and facilities, and calls for safe passage for civilians wishing to leave areas affected by hostilities;
7. Condemns in the strongest terms all attacks against MONUSCO personnel, installations, vehicles and equipment, against SAMIDRC personnel and assets, and against humanitarian personnel, and demands that all parties respect the safety and security of United Nations and associated personnel, ensure freedom of movement and access for MONUSCO and SAMIDRC, and comply fully with the Status of Forces Agreement;
8. Reaffirms MONUSCO’s mandate, including the protection of civilians, and requests MONUSCO to continue to support SAMIDRC in accordance with resolution 2746 (2024), within existing resources and within the area of its deployment, and stresses that such support should be provided without prejudice to MONUSCO’s mandate and in strict compliance with the United Nations Human Rights Due Diligence Policy;
9. Calls for enhanced coordination, information-sharing and deconfliction between MONUSCO, SAMIDRC, the Armed Forces of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and other relevant actors in order to protect civilians and facilitate humanitarian access, and stresses that all operations should be conducted in full compliance with international law, including international humanitarian law and international human rights law, as applicable;
10. Emphasizes that those responsible for violations of international humanitarian law and international human rights law, including massacres, conflict-related sexual violence, attacks against peacekeepers and humanitarian personnel and obstruction of humanitarian access, must be held accountable, and calls upon the authorities of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and all relevant States to cooperate in investigations and accountability efforts, including with the United Nations Joint Human Rights Office and the Group of Experts;
11. Reiterates its full support for the Luanda and Nairobi processes, calls for the resumption of direct dialogue between the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Rwanda, and urges the parties to implement fully the ceasefire of 30 July 2024 and the Luanda commitments, including through the establishment of appropriate verification and monitoring mechanisms and the neutralization of the FDLR, and welcomes the continued mediation of Angola and the engagement of the African Union, the East African Community, the Southern African Development Community and the United Nations;
12. Calls upon the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Rwanda to de-escalate tensions, restore diplomatic channels, refrain from hostile rhetoric and unilateral actions, respect the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of all States in the region, and engage in good faith in diplomatic dialogue to address security concerns and achieve a lasting political settlement;
13. Calls upon all Member States and international partners to provide humanitarian assistance and support to affected populations and to reinforce regional peace efforts, and underlines the need for predictable and adequate resources to respond to the humanitarian crisis in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo;
14. Expresses its readiness to consider additional measures, including targeted sanctions, against individuals and entities responsible for undermining peace and security in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo, including those responsible for violations and abuses of human rights and international humanitarian law, attacks against peacekeepers and humanitarian personnel, obstruction of humanitarian access, or direct or indirect support to armed groups, and requests the Group of Experts to continue to provide information on such support in its reporting;
15. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council within thirty days on the implementation of this resolution, including on the humanitarian situation, the safety and security of MONUSCO and humanitarian personnel, MONUSCO’s support to SAMIDRC consistent with resolution 2746 (2024), and options for strengthened coordination among regional and international efforts, and further requests the Special Envoy of the Secretary-General for the Great Lakes Region to brief the Council in a timely manner;
16. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

