Recognizing the linkage between the illegal exploitation of natural resources, illicit trade in such resources and the proliferation and trafficking of arms as one of the major factors fuelling and exacerbating conflict in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo,
Welcoming the efforts of the African Union, the East African Community, the Southern African Development Community, the International Conference on the Great Lakes Region, Angola as mediator, and the United Nations, including the Special Envoy of the Secretary-General for the Great Lakes Region, and recalling the ceasefire agreement signed by the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Rwanda on 30 July 2024 under the mediation of Angola, as well as the Luanda commitments and the decision to operationalize the FDLR neutralization plan and to elaborate a disengagement plan,
Reaffirming the mandate of MONUSCO, including the protection of civilians, and recalling resolution 2746 (2024), which authorizes MONUSCO to support SAMIDRC through enhanced coordination, information-sharing, technical assistance and the use of MONUSCO’s logistical assets and military capabilities, within the area of MONUSCO’s deployment and consistent with resolution 2717 (2023),
Stressing the importance of coordinated, complementary and effective action by the United Nations, the African Union, the East African Community, the Southern African Development Community and other regional and international partners in support of a lasting political settlement,
Determining that the situation in the Democratic Republic of the Congo continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,
Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,
1. Demands the immediate cessation of all hostilities and the immediate and unconditional withdrawal of M23 from all areas of the Democratic Republic of the Congo that it occupies, including Goma and Bukavu;
2. Demands that all external parties immediately cease all support to M23, including the provision of military personnel, weapons, equipment, logistics and operational support, and immediately and unconditionally withdraw all foreign military forces operating in the Democratic Republic of the Congo without its consent, without delay, in full respect for the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and all States in the region;
3. Condemns all direct and indirect support to armed groups operating in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, and demands that all States and actors cease such support, prevent the transfer of arms and related materiel to armed groups, and comply strictly with the sanctions regime established by resolution 1533 (2004) and subsequent resolutions;
4. Calls upon all States and relevant international and regional organizations to strengthen cooperation in tracking financial flows linked to the illegal exploitation and trade of natural resources in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo, and requests the Group of Experts to include such information in its reporting;
5. Demands that all armed groups operating in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, including M23, the Allied Democratic Forces, CODECO and the FDLR, immediately cease all violence, disarm, demobilize and participate in good faith in disarmament, demobilization, reintegration and stabilization processes, and calls for the full implementation of the FDLR neutralization plan;
6. Calls upon all parties to respect international humanitarian law and international human rights law, to protect civilians, including women, children, internally displaced persons and refugees, to prevent and end conflict-related sexual violence and grave violations against children, and to refrain from attacks against civilian objects, including hospitals, schools, water and sanitation facilities, and humanitarian and medical infrastructure;
7. Demands that all parties facilitate safe, timely and unimpeded humanitarian access, in accordance with the humanitarian principles of humanity, neutrality, impartiality and independence, to all affected populations, including in Goma, Bukavu and other areas affected by the recent offensives, across front lines and borders, and to populations in areas controlled by armed groups, and ensure the safety and security of humanitarian personnel, medical personnel, humanitarian convoys and facilities, and calls for safe passage for civilians wishing to leave areas affected by hostilities, including refugees and internally displaced persons, and demands that all parties respect the principle of non-refoulement;
8. Condemns in the strongest terms all attacks against MONUSCO personnel, installations, vehicles and equipment, against SAMIDRC personnel and assets, and against humanitarian personnel, and demands that all parties respect the safety and security of United Nations and associated personnel, ensure freedom of movement and access for MONUSCO and SAMIDRC, and comply fully with the Status of Forces Agreement;
9. Reaffirms MONUSCO’s mandate, including the protection of civilians, and requests MONUSCO to continue to support SAMIDRC in accordance with resolution 2746 (2024), within existing resources and within the area of its deployment, and stresses that such support should be provided without prejudice to MONUSCO’s mandate and strategic priorities, without affecting its capacity to implement its mandate, excluding MONUSCO’s involvement in combat operations, and consistent with resolution 2746 (2024) and applicable United Nations policies, including the United Nations Human Rights Due Diligence Policy;
10. Calls for enhanced coordination, information-sharing and deconfliction between MONUSCO, SAMIDRC, the Armed Forces of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and other relevant actors in order to protect civilians and facilitate humanitarian access, and stresses that all operations should be conducted in full compliance with international law, including international humanitarian law and international human rights law, as applicable;
11. Stresses that any further adjustments to MONUSCO’s deployment must be conditions-based, sequenced and conducted in close consultation with the Government of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and troop-contributing countries, and must not create a security vacuum or undermine the protection of civilians;
12. Emphasizes that those responsible for violations of international humanitarian law and international human rights law, including massacres, conflict-related sexual violence, attacks against peacekeepers and humanitarian personnel and obstruction of humanitarian access, must be held accountable, and calls upon the authorities of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and all relevant States to cooperate in investigations and accountability efforts, including with the United Nations Joint Human Rights Office, the Group of Experts and, as applicable, the International Criminal Court;
13. Reiterates its full support for the Luanda and Nairobi processes, including the Nairobi process’s role in inclusive dialogue with local armed groups and the Luanda process’s role in DRC–Rwanda dialogue, calls for the resumption of direct dialogue between the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Rwanda, and urges the parties to implement fully the ceasefire of 30 July 2024 and the Luanda commitments, including through MONUSCO’s continued support for the Luanda process, the full operationalization of the reinforced ad hoc verification mechanism agreed under the Luanda process, the establishment of appropriate verification and monitoring mechanisms and the neutralization of the FDLR, welcomes the continued mediation of Angola and the engagement of the African Union and its Peace and Security Council, the East African Community, the Southern African Development Community and the United Nations, and stresses the importance of the full, equal, safe and meaningful participation of women in all peace and political processes, including the Luanda and Nairobi processes;
14. Calls upon the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Rwanda to de-escalate tensions, restore diplomatic channels, refrain from hostile rhetoric and unilateral actions, respect the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of all States in the region, and engage in good faith in diplomatic dialogue to address security concerns and achieve a lasting political settlement;
15. Calls upon all Member States and international partners to provide humanitarian assistance and support to affected populations and to reinforce regional peace efforts, and underlines the need for predictable and adequate resources to respond to the humanitarian crisis in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo;
16. Requests the Group of Experts to continue to provide information on support to armed groups in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo in its reporting, including on direct or indirect support to M23 by external actors, for the consideration of the Committee established pursuant to resolution 1533 (2004) in accordance with its procedures; expresses its readiness to consider, as appropriate and on the basis of credible and verifiable information, targeted measures, including targeted sanctions under the sanctions regime established by resolution 1533 (2004) and subsequent resolutions, against individuals and entities responsible for or complicit in actions that threaten the peace, security or stability of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, including direct or indirect support to M23, in full respect of the Charter of the United Nations, due process and the principle of non-interference, and in close consultation with the African Union and regional mechanisms, and reaffirms that any such consideration should not substitute for political dialogue; and stresses that such consideration should include violations and abuses of human rights and international humanitarian law, including conflict-related sexual violence and grave violations against children, attacks against peacekeepers and humanitarian personnel, obstruction of humanitarian access, and direct or indirect support to armed groups;
17. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council within sixty days on the implementation of this resolution, including on the humanitarian situation, the safety and security of MONUSCO and humanitarian personnel, MONUSCO’s support to SAMIDRC consistent with resolution 2746 (2024), including on steps taken to ensure MONUSCO’s impartiality and non-involvement in combat operations and on the impact of MONUSCO’s support to SAMIDRC on its mandate, on compliance by all relevant parties with operative paragraph 2 and progress in implementing commitments under the Luanda process, including on reports of external military support and the withdrawal of foreign military forces operating in the Democratic Republic of the Congo without its consent, and options for strengthened coordination among regional and international efforts, and further requests the Special Envoy of the Secretary-General for the Great Lakes Region to brief the Council in a timely manner;
18. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.
